  "notes": "Built from the real archived 2023-2024 bulletin, fetched directly from https://bulletins.psu.edu/archive/2023-2024/undergraduate/colleges/liberal-arts/economics-bs/ -- its 'Program Requirements' (Prescribed Courses ECON 102/104/106/302/304/306, Additional Courses CMPSC 101-or-203 + MATH 110-or-140, and Supporting Courses: 3cr social/behavioral, 6cr arts/humanities/social-behavioral, 3cr quantification, 18cr ECON at 300/400 level with a minimum of 9cr at 400-level) are worded identically to the 2026 edition -- confirmed by fetching the archive page directly, not assumed. The real difference from 2026: this edition's Suggested Academic Plan has no First-Year/Second-Year Seminar (no LA 83/LA 283 line), giving a 120-credit total instead of 2026's 123 -- the seminars were added starting with the 2025-26 edition (see ECON-2025.json). Genuine finding worth flagging: unlike ECON-2026.json (whose own notes describe finding only 12cr of the real 18cr ECON-elective requirement modeled, needing a fix), this edition's own printed Suggested Academic Plan already explicitly labels enough slots (one '300 Level ECON', two '300/400 Level ECON', three '400 Level ECON') to total the full, correct 18cr with 9cr guaranteed at 400-level -- no analogous fix was needed here; it was modeled directly off what the archive page prints. ECON 106's 'MATH 21' prerequisite is the same PSU placement-threshold quirk 2026 already documents (MATH 21 needs MATH 4, needs MATH 3) -- this comes from econ_catalog.json's scraped course descriptions, which are university-wide and not year-specific, so the exact same MATH 3 -> MATH 4 -> MATH 21 chain from ECON-2026.json is added explicitly here too (Semesters 1-3), on top of the printed plan, same convention as 2026. Supporting Courses' departmental-list categories stay generic, unenumerated slots for the same reason documented in ECON-2026.json's notes: no department handbook beyond the bulletin was found, and the bulletin itself never enumerates those lists. Slot credits (GEN ED, generic Elective) are approximations of the printed plan's per-semester totals and can be edited freely, same convention as every other plan in this codebase.",
